---
import Code from '../../components/Code.astro'
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'

/**
 * Plain JavaScript, as its own page.
 *
 * It was a section on /docs/frameworks called "Qwik, Astro, Lit, or no
 * framework", which puts the one case that needs no binding at all at the
 * bottom of a page about bindings. Someone evaluating a headless editor for a
 * page with no framework should not have to read past four of them first.
 */
---

<Docs
  title="JavaScript rich text editor | Matra docs"
  description="Build a rich text editor in plain JavaScript with Matra: no framework, no build step, three calls. Mount, listen, read the document out."
  section="Frameworks"
  heading="Plain JavaScript"
>
  <p>
    No binding, no build step, no framework. The core is the whole thing · the bindings that
    exist for React, Vue, Svelte and Solid only wrap what is on this page.
  </p>

  <Code code={`npm i @matrajs/core`} lang="sh" />

  <h2>An editor</h2>
  <Code
    code={`import { createEditor, starterKit } from '@matrajs/core'

const editor = createEditor({
  extensions: starterKit,
  content: '<p>Hello.</p>',
  element: document.querySelector('#editor'),
})`}
    lang="ts"
  />
  <p>
    <code>element</code> mounts it immediately. Leave it out and you get an unmounted editor to
    <code>editor.mount(el)</code> yourself later, which is what the bindings do when the element does
    not exist until after a render.
  </p>

  <h2>Without a bundler</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>&lt;script type="module"&gt;</code> and a CDN is enough. Every editor on this site is
    exactly that.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`<div id="editor"></div>
<script type="module">
  import { createEditor, starterKit } from 'https://esm.sh/@matrajs/core'

  createEditor({
    extensions: starterKit,
    content: '<p>Hello.</p>',
    element: document.querySelector('#editor'),
  })
</script>`}
    lang="html"
  />

  <h2>A toolbar</h2>
  <p>
    The two things worth knowing without a binding: <code>editor.on()</code> returns its own unsubscribe,
    and <code>editor.isActive()</code> reads the answer out of the document rather than out of a variable
    you have to keep in step.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`const bold = document.querySelector('#bold')

bold.addEventListener('click', () => {
  editor.commands.toggleBold()
  editor.focus()
})

const off = editor.on('selectionChange', () => {
  bold.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(editor.isActive('bold')))
})`}
    lang="ts"
  />
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <code>editor.focus()</code> after a command, always. A click on a button moves focus to the
      button, and a command that runs with the caret gone looks like a command that did nothing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reading it back</h2>
  <Code
    code={`editor.getJSON()   // the document · store this
editor.getHTML()   // markup
toMarkdown(editor.getJSON())`}
    lang="ts"
  />
  <p>
    Store the JSON, not the HTML. HTML has to be parsed back through the schema on the way in,
    and anything the schema does not recognise is dropped · JSON round-trips exactly.
  </p>

  <h2>Tearing it down</h2>
  <Code
    code={`off()             // any listener you registered
editor.destroy()  // the editor, its view and its plugins`}
    lang="ts"
  />
  <p>
    <code>destroy()</code> leaves the element where it was and safe to mount into again. Not calling
    it on a page that swaps editors in and out is the one leak worth watching for.
  </p>

  <h2>On a server</h2>
  <p>
    None of this needs a browser. <code>createEditor</code> builds a document with no view, and
    <a href="/docs/extension-reference"><code>renderToHTML</code></a> skips the editor entirely ·
    both run in Node, in a worker and at the edge.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`import { renderToHTML, starterKit } from '@matrajs/core'

// no editor, no DOM, no schema build
const html = renderToHTML(storedJson, starterKit)`}
    lang="ts"
  />
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      One condition on a server: <strong>load JSON or Markdown, not an HTML string</strong>.
      Reading HTML is a DOM job, so <code>content: '&lt;p&gt;…&lt;/p&gt;'</code> needs a browser while
      <code>content: storedJson</code> and <code>content: fromMarkdown(text)</code> do not. Writing
      HTML out works either way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>TypeScript</h2>
  <p>
    Commands are inferred from the extensions you pass, so this is a compile error rather than a
    silent no-op at run time — with no generics to thread and nothing to declare.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`const editor = createEditor({ extensions: [document, paragraph, text] })

editor.commands.toggleHeading(2)
//              ^ no extension defines this`}
    lang="ts"
  />
</Docs>
